<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet>
    <title>Lab: Method of Characteristics and Shock Waves</title>
    

    <introduction>
        <p>
        <alert>MTH 401
        </alert>
    </p>
    </introduction>
     <p>
    We're going to see an application of the wave equation, in traffic!
    Specifically, we're going to determine how traffic is actually a
    <alert>shock wave</alert>!
     </p>
     <assemblage>
        <title>Method of Characteristics</title>
        
        <p>
            A technique for solving specific PDEs, reducing a PDE to to a 
            family of ODEs which can then be solved along what are called 
            <q>characteristic curves</q> that we can then transform into a 
            solution of the original PDE.
        </p>
        </assemblage>

<paragraphs>
    <title>An example with a constant <q>speed</q> <m>c</m></title>
    

        <p>
            Let's consider again the wave equation in one dimension (rearranged):
            <md>
                \pdtwo{u}{t} - c^2 \pdtwo{u}{x} = 0.
            </md>
            Note that <m>c</m> is a constant!  (We won't use <m>v</m> here to avoid confusion later.) We do a little algebra trick to <q>factor</q> this
            <md>
               <mrow> (<fillin characters="5" />)(<fillin characters="5"/>) \amp = 0</mrow>
               <mrow> (<fillin characters="5"/>)(<fillin characters="3"/>)\amp = 0 </mrow>

            </md>
            and we end up at an alternative view of the wave equation: 
        </p>
        <p workspace="1in">
            
        </p>
        <p>
            Fix a constant output <m>w(x,t) = w(x(t),t)=h_0</m> and determine the path <m>{x(t)}</m>
            with some initial <m>x(0)=x_0</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
            If <m>w(x(t),t) = h_0</m> is constant, then we know
            <md>
                <fillin characters="8" /> = <fillin characters="2" />
            </md>
         Multivariable calculus to the rescue!
         <md>
            \frac{d}{dt}(w(x(t),t)) = \pd{w}{t} +\frac{dx}{dt} \pd{w}{x} = <fillin characters="2" />.
         </md>
         </p>
         <p>

            Looking back at the alternative view of the wave equation, this means 
            <md>
                \frac{dx}{dt} = <fillin characters="3" />
            </md>
            which has the general solution:
            <md>
                x(t)   = <fillin characters="5" /> 
            </md>
            but the initial condition <m>x(0)=x_0</m> means the solution becomes
            <md>
                x(t) = <fillin characters="5" />.
            </md>
            
        </p>
        <p workspace="1in">
            Let's draw these characteristics and an example.

        </p>

    </paragraphs>
    <paragraphs>
        <title>A non-linear example</title>
        <p>
            Now something a little more interesting, and an example frequent in real-life:
            <md>
                \pd{u}{t} + u \pd{u}{x} = 0.
            </md>
            Note that the term <m>u\pd{u}{x}</m> is non-linear, so the super-position 
            principle doesn't work.  Let's again try the Method of Characteristics and look for a path <m>x(t)</m> 
            that starts at <m>{x(0)=x_0}</m> and where
            <m>u</m> is a constant, i.e. <m>{u(x(t),t) = u_0}</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
            Calc 3 and derivative knowledge tells us that 
            <md>
                \frac{d}{dt}[u(x(t),t)] = \pd{u}{t} + \frac{dx}{dt}\pd{u}{x} = 0
            </md>
            so comparing our initial equation we know 
            <md>
                \frac{dx}{dt} = <fillin characters="5" /> = <fillin characters="5" />
            </md>
            which has the solution:
            <md>
                x(t) = <fillin characters="8" />
            </md>
            Since <m>\frac{dx}{dt}</m> is the <fillin characters="5" />, it 
            depends directly on <fillin characters="30" />.
        </p>
        <definition xml:id="def-dispersion">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <term>Dispersion</term> is the physical phenomenon where things travel at different speeds.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>
        <p workspace="1in">
            Example of dispersion:
            

        </p>
        <definition xml:id="def-ShockWave">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A <term>shock wave</term> occurs when a discontinuity happens, that 
                    is when different characteristics meet, and we have a <term>sonic boom</term>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>
       
        <p workspace="in">
            Shock wave example:
        </p>

         <p>
            See a <url href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=If-yK7sQE8Q">Shock Wave Video Explanation</url> and 
            <url href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BUREX8aFbMs">Sonic Boom Volcano</url>
        </p>
    </paragraphs>



<exploration>
    <title>Traffic!</title>
    
    
  <p>
    Let's take a 1-way highway, and look at a stretch of road.
  </p>


  <image width="40%">
      <shortdescription>
        A stretch of one-way highway.
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-401-lab-highway">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,50)" margins="7">

          <coordinates bbox="(0,0,12,2)">
              <line endpoints="((0,0),(12,0))"/>
              <line endpoints="((0,2),(12,2))"/>
              <line endpoints="((5,1),(7,1))" arrows="1" thickness="1"/>
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
  <p>
    We'll set up the problem below and use the Method of Characteristics to solve it!
  </p>
  </exploration>

  <p>
    Define the following:
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          <m>\rho(x,t)</m> as density of cars at position <m>x</m> at time
          <m>t</m>, units = <fillin characters="8"/>
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <m>v(x,t)</m> as velocity of the cars at position <m>x</m> at time
          <m>t</m>, units = <fillin characters="8"/>
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
    So a car that begins at <m>x</m> ends up at <m>x+v \Delta t</m> after some
    short time interval <m>\Delta t</m>.  (We'll assume all cars 
    at position <m>x</m> have the same speed in a multi-lane highway.  Note: this is not the movement of individual cars, but the density which
    is an aggregated measure of all the cars positions.)
  </p>

  <p>
    Then the product:
  </p>

  <p>
    <m>\rho(x,t) v(x,t)</m> is <fillin characters="40"/>,
    units = <fillin characters="8"/>
  </p>

  <p>
    We're going to focus on the density function, <m>\rho(x,t)</m>, and come
    up with a mathematical model.  We find something useful if we consider the
    number <m>N</m> of cars in an interval <m>\left[a,b\right]</m>.
  </p>

  <image width="40%">
      <shortdescription>
        A stretch of one-way highway from a to b.
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-401-lab-highway2">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,50)" margins="7">

          <coordinates bbox="(0,-1,12,2)">
              <line endpoints="((0,0),(12,0))"/>
              <line endpoints="((0,2),(12,2))"/>
              <rectangle lower-left="(2,0)" dimensions="(8,2)"
               fill="pink" />
               <label anchor="(2,-0.5)"><m>a</m> </label>
               <label anchor="(10,-0.5)"><m>b</m> </label>
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>

  <p>
    We can get <m>N</m> by integrating the density over the interval
    <m>\left[a,b\right]</m>:
    <md>
      <mrow>N(t) = \int_a^b \rho(x,t) \,dx.</mrow>
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    If <m>N</m> is the total number of cars in the interval, then the rate of
    change of the number of cars in this interval is (switch the order of the
    operators and put everything under an integral):
    <md>
      <mrow>\frac{dN}{dt} = <fillin characters="12"/> = <fillin characters="12"/></mrow>
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    We can approach this same quantity from a different perspective, a law of
    conservation.  The rate of change of the number of cars in
    <m>\left[a,b\right]</m> is the difference in the rate at which cars enter
    the interval on the left at <m>a</m>, and the rate at which cars leave on
    the right at <m>b</m>.  Can we write this also as an integral from
    <m>a</m> to <m>b</m>?
    <md>
      <mrow>\frac{dN}{dt} = (\rho v)|_{x=a} - (\rho v)|_{x = b} =  -\left((\rho v)|_{x=b} - (\rho v)|_{x = a}\right) = <fillin characters="12"/></mrow>
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    Altogether we have
    <md>
      <mrow>\frac{dN}{dt} = \int_a^b \frac{\partial \rho}{\partial t} \,dx = -  \int_a^b \frac{\partial (\rho v)}{\partial x} \,dx.</mrow>
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    Rearrange two parts of the equation above to get an integral equal to 0.
    <md>
      <mrow><fillin characters="20"/> = 0</mrow>
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    Since this is true for EVERY interval <m>\left[a,b\right]</m>, then it
    must be true that the integrand itself is zero, and we end up with the
    following equation:
    <md>
      <mrow><fillin characters="20"/> = 0</mrow>
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    This is the partial differential equation we want to focus on. And we can
    think of the density function as a wave, the highway is like our string,
    and the value of the density function is like the height of the string.
    Of course there is a maximum density of traffic on the road, but we can
    divide by the maximum density and thus we can assume here that
    <m>0\le \rho\le 1</m>.
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          <m>\rho = </m> <fillin characters="4"/> corresponds to max density:
          bumper-to-bumper traffic.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <m>\rho \approx </m> <fillin characters="4"/> corresponds to min
          density: almost no cars on the road.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <m>\rho = \frac14</m> may correspond to an example such as
          <fillin characters="28"/>
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </p>

  <p>
    Let's examine velocity here...  It is certainly reasonable to assume
    velocity <m>v</m> depends on density of traffic <m>\rho</m>.  When there
    are no cars on the road (<m>\rho  = </m> <fillin characters="4"/>),
    traffic will move at its maximum speed.  Let
    <md>
      <mrow>c = \text{ max speed of traffic.}</mrow>
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    We expect that when density increases, the speed <m>v</m> will decrease,
    and that when there is bumper-to-bumper traffic,
    (<m>{\rho = <fillin characters="4"/>}</m>), no one is moving and so
    <m>{v =  <fillin characters="4"/>}</m>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Draw a linear possibility on the
    graph below and determine the equation for <m>v</m> as a function of <m>\rho</m> that corresponds
    to it.
  </p>

  <sidebyside widths="30% 40%" margins="auto" valign="middle">
    <image>
      <shortdescription>
        Blank axes with density on the horizontal axis and velocity on the vertical axis.
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-401-lab-velocity">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,200)" margins="13">

          <coordinates bbox="(-1.2,-0.3,1.7,1.5)">
            <axes xlabel="density" ylabel="velocity" vlabels="(3,5,5)"/>
            <point p="(0,.8)" alignment="w"><m>c = </m>max velocity</point>
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
    <p>
      <m>v(\rho) = </m> <fillin characters="20"/>
    </p>
  </sidebyside>

  <p>
    This may seem like an overly simplistic assumption, but Lighthill and
    Whitham describe experimental evidence that justifies its use.  (See
    references.)
  </p>

  <p>
    Ok, we have an equation for <m>v</m>.  Let's plug that in to our PDE.  We
    now have:
    <md>
      <mrow>\frac{\partial \rho}{\partial t} + \frac{\partial (c(1-\rho)\rho)}{\partial x} = 0.</mrow>
    </md>
    Calculus to the rescue, our final expression is:
    <md>
      <mrow>\frac{\partial \rho}{\partial t} + c(1-2\rho) \frac{\partial \rho}{\partial x} = 0.</mrow>
    </md>
  </p>


<aside>
    <p>
        Quick return to the Method of Characteristics:
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Assume density <m>\rho</m> is constant, so <m>\rho(x(t),t) = \rho(x_0,t) = \rho_0</m>.

                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    (Think of density <m>\rho</m> as a wave; we look for the path of 
                    the point starting at <m>{(x_0,\rho_0)}</m> that doesn't change 
                    the height of the wave, like a level curve...)
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    We want to track the path <m>x(t)</m> that keeps <m>\rho=\rho_0</m> constant, that is 
                    <m>{\rho(x(t),t) = \rho_0}</m>.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Calculus tells us the derivative in time must be 0, that is:
                    <md>
                        \pd{\rho}{t} + \frac{dx}{dt}\pd{\rho}{x} = 0.
                    </md>
                    
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </p>
</aside>
  

  
   
  <p>
    So matching the equations, we know:
    <md>
      <mrow>\frac{dx}{dt} =  <fillin characters="16"/></mrow>
    </md>
    This is the speed of our density "wave"!
  </p>

  <p>
    Because we are looking for where <m>\rho(x(t),t) = \rho_0</m>, we can sub
    in <m>\rho_0</m> for <m>\rho</m>, meaning that the path our point takes is
    <md>
      <mrow>x(t) = c(1-2\rho_0)t + x_0.</mrow>
    </md>
    So an initial point <m>x_0</m> moves to the left or right with speed
    <m>{\frac{dx}{dt}=c(1-2\rho_0)}</m>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Note the difference between <q>speeds</q>:
    <ul>
        <li>
            <p>
                <m>c</m> is the max velocity possible of the cars,
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                <m>\frac{dx}{dt} = c(1-2\rho_0)</m> is the speed of the wave.
            </p>
        </li>
    </ul>
  </p>

  <p>
    <alert>Context check:</alert>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          If <m>\rho_0 = 0</m> (min density), <m>\frac{dx}{dt} = <fillin characters="2" /></m>, then <m>x_0</m> moves with
          speed <fillin characters="8"/> to the <fillin characters="8"/>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          If <m>\rho_0 = 1/2</m> (middle density), <m>\frac{dx}{dt} = <fillin characters="2" /></m>, then <m>x_0</m> moves <fillin characters="8"/>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          If <m>\rho_0 = 1</m> (maximum density), <m>\frac{dx}{dt} = <fillin characters="2" /></m>, then <m>x_0</m> moves with
          speed <fillin characters="8"/> to the <fillin characters="8"/>.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </p>

  <p>
    At what initial density do we move to the right as fast as possible?
    <fillin characters="8"/>
  </p>

  <p>
    At what initial density do we move to the left as fast as possible?
    <fillin characters="8"/>
  </p>

  <p>
    At initial densities <m>0&lt;\rho_0 &lt; 1/2</m>, does the initial
    <m>x_0</m> move to the right or left?  <fillin characters="8"/>
  </p>

  <p>
    At initial densities <m>1/2&lt;\rho_0 &lt; 1</m>, does the initial
    <m>x_0</m> move to the right or left?  <fillin characters="8"/>
  </p>

  <p>
    Since the speed of our density
    <q>wave</q> depends on <m>\rho_0</m>, we need an initial density
    <q>wave</q>, i.e. an initial curve for <m>\rho(x,0)</m>.  Let's look at some examples and apply what we know!
  </p>

<exploration>
    <title>Shear Rule Example</title>
    <p>
        Let's look at the following initial density curve. Draw arrows on the curve at various points indicating 
        if the x-coordinate will move left or right with a speed of <m>c(1-\rho_0)</m>.
        Make sure the lengths of your arrows represent the speed (faster should 
        correspond to longer arrows). Connect the tips of the arrows to show how the wave changes over time.  On the right, 
        draw the characteristics.

    </p>

    <sidebyside widths="40% 40%">
        <image>
      <shortdescription>
        An initial density curve with x on the horizontal axis and density on the vertical axis.
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-401-lab-density1">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,150)" margins="7">
          <definition>f(x)=1/(1+e^(ln(200)*(x-1)))</definition>
          <coordinates bbox="(-1/2,-1/2,2.2,1.3)">
            <axes xlabel="x" ylabel="\rho_0" vlabels="(0,1/2,5)" hlabels="(3,3,5)" hticks="(0,1,1)"/>
            <graph function="f"/>
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
    <image>
      <shortdescription>
        A blank graph with x-axis x and y-axis t to draw the characteristics.
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-401-lab-char1">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,150)" margins="7">
          <coordinates bbox="(-1,-1/2,2.2,1.3)">
            <axes decorations="no" xlabel="x" ylabel="t" />
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
    </sidebyside>
    <p>
        Any guesses as to why it's called the Shear Rule?
    </p>
    <p>
        Think about a situation where everyone is stopped at a traffic light,
        as shown below.
      </p>
      <image source="images/stop_1.png" width="50%">
        <shortdescription>Cars stopped at a traffic light, with density 1 before the light and 0 past it.</shortdescription>
      </image>
      <p>
        Density past the light is 0 (cars have cleared out), and before the
        light is 1 (bumper-to-bumper, no one is moving).  According to
        intuition AND the method of characteristics, once the light turns
        green, what would happen to this density <q>wave</q>?  Draw the
        <q>wave</q> at a later time.
      </p>
</exploration>


  <exploration>
    <title>Shock Wave Example</title>
    <p>
        Well that's cool and all, but I did promise you shock waves.
        Consider the following initial density curve, <m>\rho(x,0)</m>, where
        now our curve is increasing.  Just like before, draw in arrows on the 
        density curve to indicate direction, and on the right draw characteristics.
      </p>

      <sidebyside widths="50% 40%">
      
      <image width="40%">
      <shortdescription>
        An initial density curve with x on the horizontal axis and density on the vertical axis.
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-401-lab-density2">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,150)" margins="7">
          <definition>f(x)=1/(1+e^(-ln(200)*(x-1)))</definition>
          <coordinates bbox="(-1/2,-1/2,2.2,1.3)">
            <axes xlabel="x" ylabel="\rho_0" vlabels="(0,1/2,5)" hlabels="(3,3,5)" hticks="(0,1,1)"/>
            <graph function="f"/>
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
 <image>
      <shortdescription>
        A blank graph with x-axis x and y-axis t to draw the characteristics.
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-401-lab-char2">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,150)" margins="7">
          <coordinates bbox="(-1,-1/2,2.2,1.3)">
            <axes decorations="no" xlabel="x" ylabel="t" />
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
      
      </sidebyside>
    <p>
        Let enough time pass, what happens?  A discontinuity!  A shock wave!
        We can draw this density curve where the discontinuity will become a vertical line:
    </p>
    <image width="35%">
      <shortdescription>
        An initial density curve with x on the horizontal axis and density on the vertical axis.
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-401-lab-density22">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,150)" margins="7">
          <coordinates bbox="(-1/2,-1/2,2.2,1.3)">
            <axes xlabel="x" ylabel="\rho_0" vlabels="(0,1/2,5)" hlabels="(3,3,5)" hticks="(0,1,1)"/>
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
    <p>
        What does this situation correspond to in real life? <fillin characters="20" />
    </p>
    <p>
        On the bottom of the discontinuity we have some density <m>\rho_1</m>, 
        and at the top we have some density <m>\rho_2</m>. The 
        shock wave moves with a speed that is the average of the speeds on either 
        side of the shock wave.  So the speed of the shock wave is 
        <md>
            \frac{c(1-2\rho_1)+c(1-2\rho_2)}{2} = c-(\rho_1+\rho_2)
        </md>
        
    </p>
  </exploration>
      
<exploration>
    <title>Shear Rule + Shock Waves</title>
    <p>
        We can combine both situations to understand what happens when a traffic 
        light changes from green to red.
    </p>
    <p>
        Suppose the density is initial constant (say around <m>1/4</m>) with 
        a green light, so 
        traffic is moving smoothly (left picture). when suddenly the light turns red. Traffic 
        past the light will keep going and clear out, but cars will pile up before the light (right picture).
    </p>
    <sidebyside widths="40% 6% 40%" margins="auto" valign="middle">
        <image source="images/stop_2.png">
          <shortdescription>Traffic just after the light turns red.</shortdescription>
        </image>
        <p><m>\rightarrow</m></p>
        <image source="images/stop_3.png">
          <shortdescription>Traffic piling up behind the red light as two shock waves form.</shortdescription>
        </image>
      </sidebyside>

      <p>
        Now let's turn the light green again.  The Shear Rule will apply to the 
        second shock wave where traffic is piled up, and the traffic (aka the shock wave)
        will begin to move to the right through the light, as pictured below.
      </p>
      <sidebyside widths="38% 6% 38%" margins="auto" valign="middle">
        <image source="images/stop_4.png">
          <shortdescription>Traffic just after the light turns green.</shortdescription>
        </image>
        <p><m>\rightarrow</m></p>
        <image source="images/stop_5.png">
          <shortdescription>The shock wave moving to the right through the light.</shortdescription>
        </image>
      </sidebyside>
      <p>
        If we turn the light red before the shock wave clears, we have reached
        a badly timed traffic light. Thus, this model can help us time the
        traffic lights so the shock clears the stop light.
      </p>
 
</exploration>

  
  <p>
    See the article by Dr. David Austin for more details about the timing of stop lights, and for animated 
    pictures:
    <url href="https://www.ams.org/publicoutreach/feature-column/fc-2019-12">You're in for a Shock</url>.
  </p>


<references xml:id="references-traffic">
  <title>References</title>

  <biblio type="raw">
    Paul Richards. Shock Waves on the Highway. Operations Research. Vol. 4,
    No. 1 (February, 1956), 42-51.
    <note>
      <p>
        Richards introduced the model for traffic flow that we considered here
        and described the shear rule and the evolution of shock waves.
      </p>
    </note>
  </biblio>

  <biblio type="raw">
    M.J. Lighthill and G.B. Whitham. On kinematics waves I. Flood movement in
    long rivers. Proceedings of the Royal Society A. 229 (1955) 281-316.
  </biblio>

  <biblio type="raw">
    M.J. Lighthill and G.B. Whitham. On kinematics waves II. A theory of
    traffic flow on long crowded roads. Proceedings of the Royal Society A.
    229 (1955) 317-345.
    <note>
      <p>
        This pair of papers presents a general theory of wave motion and
        includes traffic flow as a detailed example. The second paper
        considers the question of how the velocity depends on the density and
        provides some evidence for the model used here:
        <m>v = c(1-2\rho)</m>.
      </p>
    </note>
  </biblio>

  <biblio type="raw">
    Helge Holden and Nils Henrik Risebro. A mathematical model of traffic flow
    on a network of unidirectional roads. In Nonlinear Hyperbolic Problems:
    Theoretical, Applied, and Computational Aspects. A. Dontao, F. Oliveri
    (editors). Vieweg+Teubner Verlag. 1993.
    <note>
      <p>
        Here is a more sophisticated model of traffic flow on a network of
        interconnected roads.
      </p>
    </note>
  </biblio>

  <biblio type="raw">
    Richard Haberman. Mathematical Models: Mechanical Vibrations, Population
    Dynamics, and Traffic Flow. Society of Industrial and Applied Mathematics.
    Philadelphia. 1998.
  </biblio>

  <biblio type="raw">
    Gerald Whitham. Linear and Nonlinear Waves. Wiley. New York. 1999.
